                <div class="summary-text">
                    A geographic breakdown of living costs and purchasing power reveals significant regional patterns. While some regions show consistent affordability, others demonstrate extreme inequality and high baseline costs.
                </div>
                <div class="chart-row">
                    <div class="chart-container">{html_region1}</div>
                    <div class="chart-container">{html_region2}</div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="inflation-oil-exposure">
                <h2>Inflation and Oil Shock Exposure</h2>
                <div class="summary-text">
                    Vulnerability to external shocks, particularly oil prices and inflation, compounds the cost-of-living crisis. Cities with high oil shock exposure face significantly more volatile inflationary environments.
                </div>
                <div class="chart-row">
                    <div class="chart-container">{html_inf1}</div>
                    <div class="chart-container">{html_inf2}</div>
                </div>
            </section>

            <section id="key-findings-recommendations">
                <h2>Key Findings and Policy Implications</h2>
                <div class="recommendations">
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Targeted Housing Interventions:</strong> Cities with a rent-to-salary ratio exceeding 50% require immediate policy interventions, such as zoning reform, rent stabilization, or subsidies to alleviate severe housing burdens.</li>
                        <li><strong>Purchasing Power Inequality:</strong> The significant gap between high and low purchasing power cities highlights the need for localized wage adjustments and economic diversification to support lower-income households.</li>
                        <li><strong>Vulnerability to Exogenous Shocks:</strong> Cities with high exposure to oil price volatility are currently experiencing the highest inflation rates. Policymakers must focus on energy transition and supply chain resilience.</li>
                        <li><strong>Prioritizing High-Crisis Tiers:</strong> Resources should be disproportionately directed toward cities in the highest crisis tiers, where the compounded effects of high rent, inflation, and low purchasing power are causing acute economic stress.</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </section>

        </div>
    </body>
    </html>
    '''

